{"k":"note","text":"make_pair(a=7, b=11) returns a 16-byte struct {long a; long b}. System V places the first two integer arguments in rdi and rsi; Microsoft x64 places them in rcx and rdx. Before a single instruction runs the register files already disagree: a0 -> rdi (SysV) vs rcx (Win64), a1 -> rsi vs rdx.","step":0,"stop":true,"title":"arguments arrive in different registers"}
{"k":"note","text":"movq rdi, rax stages the struct's first 8-byte chunk into the return register. Under SysV rdi held a, so rax becomes 7; under Win64 rdi is a nonvolatile register the caller never loaded, so rax becomes 0 — the same instruction, a different input, because each convention chose a different argument register.","step":1,"stop":true,"title":"the first eightbyte is staged"}
{"k":"note","text":"movq rsi, rdx stages the second chunk. SysV classifies the 16-byte return as two INTEGER eightbytes and hands it back in the pair rax:rdx, so rax:rdx = (a, b) IS the returned struct. Win64 classifies any aggregate larger than 8 bytes as MEMORY and returns it through a hidden pointer the caller passes in rcx — which is why its integer arguments start one register later, in rdx.","step":2,"stop":true,"title":"the second eightbyte, and how each ABI returns the struct"}
{"k":"note","text":"The System V AMD64 ABI splits an aggregate into 8-byte 'eightbytes' and classifies each INTEGER, SSE or MEMORY; two INTEGER eightbytes ride in rdi:rsi inbound and rax:rdx on return. Microsoft x64 has no eightbyte split — anything that is not 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes is passed and returned by reference. The register deltas above are that one rule, made mechanical.","stop":true,"title":"eightbyte classification, side by side"}
